               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Performance</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>FBGEMM: Enabling High-Performance Low-Precision Deep Learning Inference</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Deep learning models typically use single-precision (FP32) floating point data types for representing activations and weights, but a slew of recent research work has shown that computations with reduced-precision data types (FP16, 16-bit integers, 8-bit integers or even 4- or 2-bit integers) are enough to achieve same accuracy as FP32 and are much more efficient. Therefore, we designed fbgemm, a high-performance kernel library, from ground up to perform high-performance quantized inference on current generation CPUs. fbgemm achieves efficiency by fusing common quantization operations with a high-performance gemm implementation and by shape- and size-specific kernel code generation at runtime. The library has been deployed at Facebook, where it delivers greater than 2x performance gains with respect to our current production baseline.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2021-01-13</dc:date>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2101.05615</dc:identifier>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2101.05615</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-09-12 11:21:58</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <z:shortTitle>FBGEMM</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
